faqs:
  - q: "What is the difference between percentage change and percentage points?"
    a: "Percentage change measures relative growth or drop compared to the starting baseline. Percentage points measure the simple arithmetic difference between two percentage values. For example, if an interest rate moves from 5% to 7%, the increase is 2 percentage points, but a 40% percentage change."
  - q: "Why can't percentage change be calculated when starting from zero?"
    a: "The percentage change formula divides the difference by the initial value: ((New - Old) / |Old|) × 100. When the old value is zero, division by zero occurs, which is mathematically undefined."
  - q: "What is the difference between percentage change and percentage difference?"
    a: "Percentage change has a chronological direction (old value to new value). Percentage difference compares two values where neither is considered the baseline or original, dividing the absolute difference by the average of the two numbers."
  - q: "Can percentage change exceed 100%?"
    a: "Yes. Any time a quantity more than doubles, the percentage change exceeds +100%. For instance, increasing from 20 to 60 is a +200% increase."
  - q: "Can percentage decrease exceed 100%?"
    a: "For standard positive physical quantities (like prices or weight), a decrease cannot exceed 100% because dropping by 100% brings the value to zero. Decreases beyond -100% only occur when quantities move into negative numbers."
  - q: "How does the tool handle negative baseline numbers?"
    a: "The formula uses the absolute value of the baseline in the denominator: ((New - Old) / |Old|) × 100. This ensures that moving from -100 to -50 correctly registers as an increase (+50%)."
  - q: "Can I use this calculator for year-over-year (YoY) revenue calculations?"
    a: "Yes. Enter last year's revenue in the Old Value field and this year's revenue in the New Value field to compute YoY business growth."
